															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/shaggydredlocks" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1722371); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1722371&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606250468" alt="Shaggydredlocks" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1722371)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/shaggydredlocks" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1722371); return false;" >Shaggydredlocks</a></span> <span class="odate time_1507436400 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">08 Oct 2017 04:20</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3609481">
									

<p>Gonna go ahead and second a lot of what Conwell said; the worldbuilding came off strong and how Anderson's character was handled was pretty spot on. Unfortunately, I was sorely disappointed with where the story went.</p>
<p>Though they still label them as property in official context, and apparently use them as leverage, there's something quite human and actually somewhat decent in how MC&amp;D operates here:</p>
<p>They bring back a man's entire family, and allow him to return to some semblance of a normal life (up to and including the family members resuming their roles in society, not like they're being locked up and abused by cackling sadists at cocktail parties). This puts them on track to actually have the moral high ground on the SCPF for a change&#8230;which is something I can't say I've seen examined before.</p>
<p>Next you introduce the notion that MC&amp;D have certain agreements and the SCPF obligations to uphold, so that once they're in containment, you could have had <em>something</em> occur as a result of this - that is - they could have handled the issue diplomatically, and in a way that further pushes the SCPF into the villain role, where their blind ideology is brought to the fore by friggen peddles of the macabre, no less. Instead, nothing comes of the SUSEOCT, and MC&amp;D just goes gung-ho guns-a-blazing like a group of goofy CI nuts.</p>
<p>Cut to the end, where it's revealed the family man in question is himself a replicate (which was made incredibly obvious from the second paragraph of the description) and it's just matter-of-factly repeated that the SCPF should give him back&#8230;and I just can't be arsed to care about anyone by this point. This could have used, I feel, some of Jacob's perspective, or something impassioned on the part of an MC&amp;D rep to inject some kind of pathos into the end, because as it stands now, it's just a huge letdown.</p>

							</div>

								
					
